{"chunk_id": "38a2e7116ec8", "text": "This section intertwines a discourse on truth with the dramatic humiliation of Draupadi in the Kuru court. Kasyapa teaches Prahlada that truthfulness is sacred: false testimony binds a person with the 'nooses of Varuna,' and assemblies share guilt if they fail to condemn wrongdoing. Prahlada demonstrates this principle by judging fairly even against his own son, valuing virtue over affection. Vidura then urges the Kuru assembly to apply these moral principles to Draupadi\u2019s case. Yet the court remains silent. Karna orders Dussasana to drag Draupadi away, and she laments her public humiliation, asking whether she was rightfully won in the dice game. Bhishma admits the subtlety of dharma and, unable to judge clearly, warns that the Kuru dynasty\u2019s moral confusion foreshadows its destruction.\nDraupadi, dragged into the Kuru assembly after the dice game, demands to know if she was truly won, while elders hesitate and Bhishma admits the dilemma of dharma.\nThemes: humiliation of Draupadi, moral ambiguity of dharma, silence of elders, abuse of power, justice and truth", "vector": [0.0065462421625852585, -0.004313415382057428, 0.01795733906328678, -0.011191198602318764, -0.014980237931013107, 0.018728680908679962, -0.025291837751865387, 0.000371503469068557, -0.017416048794984818, -0.03410135209560394, 0.01009508315473795, 0.017064210027456284, 0.02052847295999527, 0.016780031844973564, -0.01599515974521637, 0.009113992564380169, 0.04124639928340912, -0.011597166769206524, 0.039947301149368286, -0.034399062395095825, -0.01096114981919527, -0.012003134936094284, -0.01886400207877159, 0.006116591859608889, -0.03466970846056938, 0.029337991029024124, 0.023099606856703758, -0.023952141404151917, -0.001999394968152046, -0.015129093080759048, -0.0032781956251710653, -2.6813479053089395e-05, 0.005467042326927185, -0.021259216591715813, -0.02496706321835518, 0.005264058243483305, -0.00445212097838521, -0.0005675101419910789, 0.0087553